    "result_text": "Done. Full suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`).\n\n## What I changed\n\nA single-line-of-behaviour edit to one file \u2014 the terminal step of the `POST /api/owners` pipeline, `RespondWithOwnerCreated.java`:\n\n```java\nprivate static final org.slf4j.Logger AUDIT = org.slf4j.LoggerFactory.getLogger(\"AUDIT\");\n...\nAUDIT.info(\"owner created id={} customerCode={} registrationDate={}\",\n        owner.getId(), owner.getCustomerCode(), owner.getRegistrationDate());\n```\n\n## Why here\n\nThe audit must fire *only on successful create*, and it needs the generated owner id (populated by `SaveOwner`) plus `customerCode` (set by `AssignCustomerCode`) and `registrationDate` (set by `BuildOwner`). `RespondWithOwnerCreated` is the last step, running after `save`, so all three values are present, and it runs exclusively on the success path.\n\n## Structural impact\n\nThis is the minimum-cost option under the given cost model:\n- **One file touched**, so the file multiplier is 1 (a new function class would have forced a second file \u2014 the YAML wiring \u2014 doubling the multiplier).\n- **One function changed**, and it's a single-method class, so `WMC_other = 0 \u2192 1` (the biggest lever stays at its floor).\n- The added statement introduces no branches, so `CC` stays `1`, and `changed_lines` is ~2.\n\nThe test `AcceptanceTest.coreLogsAuditOnCreate` passes (AUDIT line: `owner created id=11 customerCode=TOW-SUR-0001 registrationDate=2026-09-03`), and the full suite reports `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0`.",
